{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over temple rooftops. The air smelled of incense and matcha. I spent mornings wandering Kinkaku-ji, its golden pavilion shimmering in the pond, and afternoons lost in Nishiki Market, tasting sweet mochi, salty yakitori, and fresh uni that melted on my tongue.\n\nTokyo was a whirlwind of neon and quiet. In Shibuya, I stood in the scramble crossing, overwhelmed by the rhythm of a million footsteps. In Akihabara, I blinked at robot cafés and anime billboards, then found peace in the moss garden of Meiji Shrine, where silence felt sacred. I rode the Shinkansen to Hakone, where Mount Fuji rose like a snow-capped guardian above misty lakes. Onsen baths warmed my bones as stars blinked awake overhead.\n\nEverywhere, kindness surprised me: an elderly woman bowing as she handed me a lost umbrella; a shopkeeper patiently teaching me how to use a vending machine with three buttons in Japanese. I ate ramen at 2 a.m. in a tiny Osaka alley, slurping noodles with strangers who smiled like old friends.\n\nI didn’t buy much—just a hand-painted fan, a tin of sencha tea, and a notebook filled with scribbled haiku. But I carried home something quieter: the stillness between temple bells, the grace of a bow, the way a single cherry blossom can make even a hurried day feel holy.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me its beauty—it taught me to notice it.  \n\n(298 words)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "0", "logpro